task: gate-143-mechanism
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-06
branch: feature/gate-143-mechanism (a script + a finding; no library code)

why: matrix-kill-by-process-group names a mechanism it never verified,
  and the team already serialises on the box because of it. Two of the
  entry's own facts point away from it: TestTwoNode kills with
  procA.destroyForcibly() -- by pid, SIGKILL -- while the victim gets
  SIGTERM (143), and TestCluster spawns no OS process at all (its two
  tests are an in-process worker and a socket worker). Meanwhile
  CHANGELOG:8155 records the same signature with an ADMITTED cause:
  "three full-matrix runs were SIGTERM-killed externally (a sibling
  pkill, admitted in the room)". So the prescribed fix (kill by pid,
  setsid the worker) may fix nothing.

how: settle it before building anything. Run the matrix beside three
  sentinels that discriminate the blast radius:
    group  -- same session as the gate, no "sbt"/"java" in its argv
    named  -- own session (os.setsid), "sbt-launch" IN its argv
    plain  -- own session, neutral argv
  Each heartbeats to a file every second, so its death time is known.
  A group-directed kill takes `group` only; a `pkill -f sbt` takes
  `named` only; an indiscriminate sweep takes all three; a targeted
  kill of the gate takes none. Snapshot ps alongside.

  Deliverable: scripts/gate-sentinels.sh so anyone can reproduce, plus
  the first result recorded against the entry. NOT a fix -- the fix is
  whatever the mechanism turns out to warrant.

note: the `named` sentinel carries "sbt-launch" in its command line ON
  PURPOSE, so a sibling's pkill would take it. That also means a
  sibling's quiet-box check (pgrep -f sbt-launch) will see it while
  this runs -- which is honest, the box IS busy then.
